<meta type=article title="Apparatus-tag reconciliation fixture" author="Test Author" doi="10.1234/example" />

<section | <ref> reconciliation — kwargs, flags>

The `<ref>` tag now honors kwargs (`type`, `format`) as data attributes and
the boolean flags `+link`/`-link`, `+preview`/`-preview`.

<$$ #eqn:newton | F = ma $$>

A default ref renders as a hyperlink with hover preview enabled:
<ref @eqn:newton>.

With `-link`, the ref renders as a span (no anchor):
<ref @eqn:newton -link>.

With `-preview`, the ref renders as an anchor but the hover-preview script
will skip attachment (data-no-preview="true" stamped on the element):
<ref @eqn:newton -preview>.

With explicit type/format kwargs flowing through to data attributes:
<ref @eqn:newton type=equation format=number>.

<section | Apparatus-tag misuse — kwargs in the wrong place>

The previous section demonstrates correct usage. This fixture also includes
a top-level <config> block with a deliberate misuse — a <meta>-shaped kwarg
appearing on <config>. The lift gate emits a "did you mean <meta>?" hint
and drops the kwarg from the config map.

<config citation-style="apa" title="Mis-placed metadata kwarg" theme="classic" />
